ode at (12,4) [right] {$Q'$}; \end{tikzpicture}\]Because the trapezoid is isosceles, by symmetry $PQ$ is parallel to $AB$ and $CD$. Therefore, $\angle PAB \cong \angle APP'$ by interior angles and $\angle PAB \cong \angle PAD$ by the problem statement. Thus, $	riangle P'AP$ is isosceles with $P'P=P'A$. By symmetry, $P'DP$ is also isosceles, and thus $P'A=\dfrac{AD}{2}$. Similarly, the same thing is happening on the right side of the trapezoid, and thus $P'Q'$ is the midline of the trapezoid. Then, $PQ=P'Q'-(P'P+Q'Q)$.
Since $P'P=P'A=\dfrac{AD}{2}$, $Q'Q=Q'B=\dfrac{BC}{2}$ and $AD=BC=333$, we have $P'P+Q'Q=\dfrac{333}{2}+\dfrac{333}{2}=333$. The length of the midline of a trapezoid is the average of their bases, so $P'Q'=\dfrac{500+650}{2}=575$. Finally, $PQ=575-333= oxed{242}$.

