\end{align*}

By solving the above system of equations, \( E(4) = 16 \). The answer for the original problem is \(  rac{16}{3} \), \( 16 + 3 = 019 \).

We proceed by casework on the number of digits. Clearly the answer must have at least two digits, seeing as the maximum digit sum for a one-digit number is 9. The answer must also have less than 4 digits, because this is the AIME.

Case 1: The answer is a 2-digit number. Represent the number as \( 10a + b \), where \( 0 < a \leq 9 \) and \( 0 \leq b \leq 9 \). The conditions of the problem translated into algebra are:


 egin{align*}17 &\mid 10a + b \a + b &= 17\end{align*}

By the Euclidean Algorithm, this is equivalent to:


 egin{align*}17 &\mid 9a\end{align*}

9 is not a factor of 17, so \( 17 \mid a \). So \( a \) must be a multiple of 17, but this is impossible because of the conditions we placed on \( a \) and \( b \). (Alternatively, note that the only possible options are 89 and 98, and neither works.)

Case 2: The answer is a 3-digit number. Represent the number as \( 100a + 10b + c \), where \( 0 < a \leq 9 \) and \( 0 \leq b, c \leq 9 \). Translating the conditions again:


 egin{align*}17 &\mid 100a + 10b + c \a + b + c &= 17 \17 &\mid 99a + 9b \17 &\mid 9(11a + b) \17 &\mid 11a + b\end{align*}

Testing multiples of 17 yields \( (4, 7, 6) \) as the minimal solution for \( (a, b, c) \) and thus the answer is \(  oxed{476} \).

